# Git hygiene — logical commits, research branches, worktrees

Layout/remotes: `30-git.mdc`. This file is how to **cut** the work. Do not
`git add -A`. Do not commit on `main` for in-flight experiments.

## Logical commits

One commit = one reviewable decision. Stage **named paths** only.

Split these; never mix in one commit:

- submodule code vs parent docs vs docker vs rules vs a gitlink bump
- a frozen `thresholds.yaml` cut vs the script that reads it vs the writeup
- a failing test fix vs a new diagnostic

**Message** (subject ≤ ~72 chars, body required unless the subject is already
the whole issue):

- Subject: outcome or capability, in the existing house style.
- Body: the **issue** — what was wrong/ambiguous, why this change, what it
  does *not* claim. Not a file list.

```
# ❌ BAD
Update smear_structure.py and docs
wip
fix stuff

# ✅ GOOD (submodule)
Add smear-structure eval: live vs dead occupancy, kNN consistency, pose control.

CA0 infidelity could be dead-leak or a systematic cone. This dump-only
script freezes those cuts in thresholds.yaml before reading shares.

# ✅ GOOD (parent)
Record smear-structure: systematic cone, not dead-leak, and bump le-wm.
```

Push the **topic branch** on the submodule first (`origin exp|feat|tooling/<slug>`),
then the parent branch (docs + gitlink bump) as its **own** commits. Land
both with PRs — see Branching. Do not push concluded work straight to `main`.

## Branching — same name in both repos

Pick a prefix, then a kebab-case slug (no dates, no initials). The **full
branch string is identical** in the parent and in `ws_le-wm/le-wm`. Land with
a pull request. Do not commit concluded work on the integration branch.

| Prefix | Use for | Examples (both repos) |
|--------|---------|------------------------|
| `exp/<name>` | A research campaign / plan doc | `exp/16-fidelity`, `exp/smear-structure` |
| `feat/<name>` | A lasting capability, not a one-shot campaign | `feat/c1-anchors` (only if that gate opens) |
| `tooling/<name>` | Infra: Docker, viz, Cursor rules, scripts plumbing | `tooling/docker-runsh`, `tooling/viz-v3` |
| `fix/<name>` | Correctness, no claim change | `fix/goal-cache-key` |

`feat/lewm-phi` is a **historical** name on the submodule (merged into
`main` with a merge commit). Do not open another `feat/lewm-phi`. Do not
use `feat/` or `tooling/` for a campaign — that is `exp/`.

**PR targets** (after the work is concluded):

| Repo | Topic branch (`exp/` · `feat/` · `tooling/` · `fix/`) | Merge into |
|------|------------------------------------------------------|------------|
| parent `ws_weltmodelle` | same name | `main` |
| submodule `le-wm` | same name | `main` (`yojuna/le-wm`, **not** Lucas) |

Submodule **`upstream/main`** is Lucas. Never PR into it. Never push to
`upstream`. Our fork’s integration is **`origin/main`**.

**Order:** merge the submodule PR first → on the parent topic branch bump
the gitlink to that SHA → then merge the parent PR into `main`.

```bash
# parent (example: experiment)
git checkout main && git pull
git checkout -b exp/16-fidelity

# submodule — identical name, from our main
git -C ws_le-wm/le-wm checkout main && git -C ws_le-wm/le-wm pull
git -C ws_le-wm/le-wm checkout -b exp/16-fidelity
```

## Worktrees

Use a **sibling** worktree (not a folder inside this clone) so that
worktree's `docker/run.sh` bind-mounts **that** tree — GPU jobs and dirty
files stay isolated. Nested `.worktrees/` would not be the container cwd
(`/workspace/ws_le-wm/le-wm`).

```bash
git worktree add -b exp/16-fidelity ../ws_weltmodelle-16-fidelity main
cd ../ws_weltmodelle-16-fidelity
git submodule update --init --recursive
# then docker from THIS tree: cd docker && ./run.sh …
```

When: a GPU run is in progress here; two campaigns at once; a risky retrain
while `main` stays at the recorded SHA. Skip for a one-file typo.

Share checkpoints: symlink `ws_le-wm/stablewm` to the main tree (or set
`STABLEWM_HOME`). Do **not** share `eval_results/` across worktrees.

Remove with `git worktree remove ../ws_weltmodelle-16-fidelity` when merged
or abandoned. Do not `rm -rf` a worktree directory.
